
.TH UNLINK 1 "October 2026" "unlink" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
unlink \- remove a file using the unlink system call
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unlink
.I FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B unlink
removes the specified file by calling the
.BR unlink (2)
system call directly.
.PP
The operation removes a directory entry and decrements
the link count of the associated inode.
The file's storage is released when its link count reaches
zero and no process holds the file open.
.PP
If the file has multiple hard links, removing one link
does not affect the others.
.PP
If the specified pathname refers to a symbolic link,
the symbolic link itself is removed, not its target.
.PP
Unlike
.BR rm ,
this command accepts exactly one pathname and does not
support recursive removal, interactive confirmation,
or forced deletion.
.PP
Directories cannot normally be removed using
.BR unlink .
Use
.B rmdir
to remove an empty directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing, allowing filenames beginning with
a hyphen.
.SH EXAMPLES
Remove a file:
.PP
.RS
.B unlink file.txt
.RE
.PP
Remove a symbolic link without affecting its target:
.PP
.RS
.B unlink symlink
.RE
.PP
Remove a file whose name begins with a hyphen:
.PP
.RS
.B unlink -- -filename
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or number of arguments was specified,
or the file could not be unlinked.
.SH STANDARDS
The basic functionality conforms to POSIX
.BR unlink .
The
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
options are extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR link (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR rmdir (1),
.BR unlink (2),
.BR link (2),
.BR symlink (2)
